## The History of the Memorial

Born in Jifna, Palestine, in 1944, Alex Odeh experienced the impact of the 1967 occupation and the Nakba firsthand. After immigrating to the United States, he earned a Master‚Äôs degree in political science from California State University, Fullerton, in 1972. During the 1980s, he became the regional director of the American-Arab Anti-Discrimination Committee (ADC) on the West Coast, where he worked to combat anti-Arab discrimination, hate crimes, and stereotypes. His life was cut short on October 11, 1985, when a pipe bomb exploded at his ADC offices in Santa Ana, killing him. Although there has been suspicion regarding right-wing Jewish groups, the FBI has never officially identified or arrested anyone responsible for the bombing, leaving the case as an unsolved murder.

## Cycles of Vandalism and Resilience

For decades, the bronze dove commissioned in 1994 to honor the activist served as a target for recurring hostility. The sculpture was stolen in 1996 and again in 1997, and it has been vandalized on several other occasions, including in 2020. Though the artwork was reattached or recovered after these incidents, the physical remains of the memorial often lay strewn with brick, a testament to the persistent animosity directed toward the symbol of his work. These repeated acts of destruction underscored the vulnerability of static monuments to political or social tensions.

## A Shift Toward Participatory Memory

The transition to a living initiative began as curators and community members sought a way to keep Odeh‚Äôs legacy alive beyond the physical risks inherent in a single, static object. During a 2023 residency at the Grand Central Art Center in Santa Ana, artist Jon Rubin began working with the community to reframe the memorial‚Äôs purpose. Rubin explained that rather than focusing on a fixed monument, the project emphasizes decentralization, turning the responsibility of memory into a collective conversation. This approach involves a series of dinners, performances, and public gatherings hosted by various individuals and organizations. By inviting people into a space of hospitality and shared dialogue, the initiative aims to build inter-community justice and peace, embodying the values Odeh himself was invested in promoting. This evolution invites a community to hold the memory of an activist not in bronze, but in the ongoing relationships and practices of those who participate in the work.ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;CsX9Jptkjh7Fk2_kSKGxnl-xHSE1x5OJFcE7wE_v_jYÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkxKoiosNewswireProseProductÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;gWxaDZP5mU6FS7pI6YeUA8hzNXmnRoRgXbFKeI-Z7zgÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkx9ni:///sha-256;0ADeHRd2UBHxYVn0AwgvX1ln8jvWtPAaR68itRUVtU8ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;rATgsSCQkrwLGrG3sq63QlVZpnYYXHVKwQ8FQ0spXbYÉdpairÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉerightÅdunitÉdleftÅdstarÅdunitÇdmarkx2026-07-21T01:00:19.602ZÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bVSfRZ6GPoyy38c2stA-4CDBils-jNOzCiduoFG-U4IÉdpairÉdleftÅdstarÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÉcsumÅdunitÅdunitÇdmark`ÉdpairÉhconstantÇdbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Tsr7QzIXV2-5wdeXyd7SqZxRnnQMIu1Kk8y9YvhiUQgÉdpairÉdleftÅdstarÉcsum